    <!-- Cipher preference (Settings + the chip at the point of use) -->
    <string name="settings_cipher_title">Cipher</string>
    <string name="settings_cipher_summary">Used for NEW encryptions — messages and files alike. Received content always decrypts regardless of this choice.</string>
    <string name="cipher_chip_label">Cipher: %1$s</string>
    <string name="cipher_pref_auto">Automatic</string>
    <string name="cipher_pref_auto_desc">Turnypt measures this device once and uses the faster cipher. Recommended.</string>
    <string name="cipher_pref_aes">AES-256-GCM</string>
    <string name="cipher_pref_aes_desc">The industry standard (TLS, full-disk encryption). Fastest on devices with a hardware AES unit — virtually all phones since ~2015.</string>
    <string name="cipher_pref_chacha">ChaCha20-Poly1305</string>
    <string name="cipher_pref_chacha_desc">The mobile-friendly standard (RFC 8439). Consistently fast in pure software — ideal for older devices without hardware AES.</string>
    <string name="cipher_auto_resolved">Automatic is currently using %1$s on this device.</string>
    <string name="cipher_dialog_title">Cipher algorithm</string>
    <string name="cipher_explain_action">Which one should I choose?</string>
    <string name="cipher_explain_body">Both algorithms are authenticated (AEAD) with a 256-bit key and a 128-bit tag — the security level is identical, and Turnypt decrypts either one no matter what you pick here.\n\n• AES-256-GCM shines when the processor has a hardware AES unit: nearly every phone since around 2015 does, and there it is the fastest.\n\n• ChaCha20-Poly1305 runs at full speed in pure software and resists timing side channels by design — the better fit for older or low-end hardware.\n\n• Automatic benchmarks both once on this device and switches to the faster one, so you never have to think about it.</string>

